      "content": "The \"individual liberty\" position can indeed hold up against the critique that traditional structures have historically justified systemic inequality or exclusion. Proponents of this view can argue that the essence of individual liberty is not inherently tied to the content of the traditions themselves, but rather to the principle of allowing individuals to live their lives as they see fit, free from coercion. They can contend that while certain traditions may have been used to perpetuate injustice, the principle of individual liberty is about the right to self-determination and the ability to challenge and reform unjust traditions. They can also argue that a truly just society must be one that allows for the evolution of traditions in response to changing values and understandings of justice. This means that while traditional structures may have been flawed, the defense of individual liberty is not about preserving these structures at all costs, but about ensuring that individuals have the freedom to question, critique, and ultimately transform these structures in the pursuit of a more equitable society. The argument does not falter; rather, it evolves to acknowledge the need for continuous reflection and reform in the pursuit of justice.",
